Trim the elementary-file pass to what stock actually reads

r49's pass read every record of every file, which overshot stock badly --
206 SIM_IO exchanges on ch 0xf5 against its 121, and 190 against 167 on 0xf6:

    cmd=0xb2 file=0x6f3c   stock 0   ours 31
    cmd=0xb2 file=0x6f3b   stock 0   ours 25
    cmd=0xc0 file=0x6f07   stock 0   ours 4     (the plugin reads IMSI itself)

Stock reads each file once -- GET_RESPONSE plus a single read -- and reads
every record only of the operator-name tables, EF_PNN (0x6fc5) and EF_OPL
(0x6fc6), which is where the bulk of its volume comes from.

So read_ef() gains a data-pass cap, the list marks those two as full reads, and
the nine files the plugin reads for its own purposes (IMSI, MSISDN, 0x6f46,
0x6f60, 0x6f61, 0x6f3e, 0x6f3f, 0x6fad, 0x6fb7) come out of it: listing them
read each one twice where stock reads it once. 37 files becomes 29.

Measured on hardware at r50: ch 0xf5 206 -> 123 against stock's 121, ch 0xf6
190 -> 132 against 167.

An earlier draft of this revision also stopped an IMS context claiming the data
subscription, on the grounds that stock sends SET_PREFERRED_DATA_MODEM twice
for a whole boot where we sent it seven times. That is reverted and not part of
this patch: the `00` it removed is a frame stock does send, our `00` came from
exactly that IMS-context claim, and the boot it shipped on registered neither
stack. The frame count is still wrong and wants fixing from the other
direction -- send stock's `00` then `01` once, deliberately -- rather than by
removing a claim whose comment records a measurement behind it.

